There is a "figure of merit" that a typical
procedure shouldn't exceed twelve lines in length.
The "kid" who helped me learn to be an OOPer (in
C++, it happens) frequently created whole new C++
_classes_ in a single line, even better.

My mind doesn't work that way, all my slavish
admiration of Thoreau doesn't help me "simplify,
simplify" so procedures running thousands of lines
are more my crop and fare.  In the first case it
wouldn't be a problem for _you_ and in the last case
nothing on the planet will help _me_, I just have to
install the whole procedure into my front level
awareness at once. Luckily, I'm extremely good at
that; I have a mind that revels in complexity.

What I'm _not_ good at doing, as indicated in a
prior posting, is reading cramped text with minimal
whitespace.  I've never met anyone else who is
either, I just have vision problems that make the
issue much more critical for me.

An additional reason for breaking out complex
constructs as I do is that in the very process of
doing that, lots of errors where delimiters got put
the wrong place become highly evident, so it is the
equivalent, for the "shop of one" coder, to doing
ones own code review.

So, to answer your question directly, no, I find my
style much more help than hinderance, otherwise I
wouldn't have adopted it.

